# Ten repeat runs of this sweep pooled into one row per (strategy, RPS step).
# Same columns as <run-dir>/plot_sn_latency.dat, plus n_runs and pooled_samples.
# Written by cld_pool_sn_latency.py; read that file's docstring for the why.
#
# SOURCE     the ten <run-dir>/ directories beside this file, each already
#            summarised by plot_sn_latency.py into its own plot_sn_latency.dat.
#            Percentiles come from the "Detailed Percentile spectrum" block of
#            <run-dir>/<strategy>/<rps>.txt; every other column is the sum of the
#            ten per-run rows, taken from that script's own collect().
# TRANSFORM  percentiles: POOLED HISTOGRAM, NOT A MEAN OF THE TEN PERCENTILES.
#            Each run's spectrum is converted back to per-bucket counts by
#            differencing the cumulative TotalCount column, the counts are summed
#            across runs, and p50/p90/p99 are read off the pooled CDF by linear
#            interpolation -- the merge HdrHistogram supports via add().
#            Histograms are additive; percentiles are not, and the mean of ten
#            p99s is not the p99 of the ten runs. pooled_samples is how many
#            completed requests that pooled histogram holds.
#            counts: shortfall / timeouts / non_2xx / offered_reqs /
#            completed_reqs / duration_s are SUMMED over the ten runs, so a
#            pooled step carries ten steps' worth of load. duration_s is
#            therefore ~10x a single step, and offered_reqs = rps x duration_s
#            still holds.
#            percentages: RECOMPUTED FROM THE POOLED TOTALS (shortfall_pct =
#            pooled shortfall / pooled offered x 100), never averaged from the
#            per-run percentages.
#            flagged = yes once the pooled shortfall_pct >= 1%.
#            loss_basis is `mixed` if the ten runs did not all use the same one.
# CAVEAT     pooling widens the sample, it does not narrow the spread: a step
#            whose ten runs disagree gets one number here with no visible
#            variance. Read this file next to the per-run plot_sn_latency.dat
#            files, not instead of them.
#            Everything the per-run file warns about still applies unchanged --
#            in particular THE PERCENTILES DESCRIBE COMPLETED REQUESTS ONLY (wrk2
#            drops a timed-out request before it reaches the histogram), and
#            `timeouts` is a count of socket timeout EVENTS, not of requests, so
#            timeout_pct can exceed shortfall_pct. Read p50/p90/p99 next to
#            shortfall_pct on the same row, never on their own.
# UNITS      latency in ms; *_pct in percent of offered load; counts in requests;
#            duration_s in seconds (summed over the ten runs).
# LAYOUT     written three times over the same rows: plot_sn_latency_pooled.dat
#            holds both arms, and plot_sn_latency_pooled_istio.dat /
#            plot_sn_latency_pooled_mazu.dat hold one arm each. The per-arm files
#            are the combined file's rows split, not recomputed -- pooling is
#            per (strategy, rps) to begin with, so no number changes. They keep
#            the `strategy` column so a row read on its own still names its arm.
# strategy	rps	p50_ms	p90_ms	p99_ms	shortfall	shortfall_pct	timeouts	timeout_pct	non_2xx	non_2xx_pct	offered_reqs	completed_reqs	duration_s	loss_basis	flagged	n_runs	pooled_samples
